  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nOver the next couple of hours, showers and storms are expected \nto develop over parts of eastern South Dakota and western \nMinnesota ahead of a cold front. Cannot entirely rule out the \npossibility of strong to severe thunderstorms, although the best\nenvironment will be to the northeast further into Minnesota. \nStill, a Marginal Risk clips the northeastern-most part of the \nforecast area, and hail is the main potential severe threat. \nStrong storms that do develop in that area are expected to exit \nthe forecast area fairly quickly this afternoon. Further south, \nexpectations are that showers will remain fairly light, and just\na few hundredths of rain will fall. \n\nBehind the cold frontal passage, a high pressure center will move \ninto the area overnight tonight. After an initial shift to the \nnorthwest, winds will become light and variable overnight tonight as \nthe high pressure center moves overhead. There is some signal in \nhigh-resolution guidance for some fog development once again tonight \ndue to the light winds and clear skies promoting strong radiative \ncooling. The main area of concern for fog development is over \nsoutheastern South Dakota, particularly the James River Valley. \nConfidence in the areal coverage is still somewhat low at the \nmoment, but pockets of dense fog may reduce visibility below a mile \nat times. \n\nThe next chances for rain come on Friday into Saturday. Rainfall \nwill move in from the south beginning Friday before expanding to \ncover the entire forecast area Friday night into Saturday. Generally \nlight rain rates are expected, but perhaps a quarter of an inch or \nmore (20-40%) could fall over parts of northwestern Iowa, \nnortheastern Nebraska, and southeastern South Dakota. \n\nAnother low pressure system is set to move into the region early \nnext week, bringing renewed chances for rain into the region. Monday \nthrough early Wednesday, chances are fairly high (50-70%, increasing \nmoving from north to south across the forecast area) to see an inch \nof rainfall accumulation or more. However, there is still \nsignificant uncertainty with this system in timing, location, and \namounts. 25th/75th spread is generally around two inches for that \nsame time period, so still a lot to resolve as the system comes into \nfocus over the next couple of days.",
